Step 15.1 is complete. All four gates pass: **942 backend tests**, **280 vitest**, production build, and the docs drift check.

## What changed

**The domain contract (D30 — the last unfilled `DomainSpec`)**
`studio/tts/providers/contract.py` adds `TTSRequest`/`TTSResultPayload` per contracts §32.3, and `domains.py` fills the `tts` `request_model`/`result_model` that were the only two left blank. The request collapses the `voice`/`tts_voice` split (P5) into one field at the boundary, rejects unknown keys, and rejects an `output_basename` that could escape its directory.

**Exclusive execution, derived rather than hardcoded**
`providers_common/concurrency.py` keys one re-entrant lock per `(domain, provider_id)`; Kokoro's manifest declares `exclusive_execution` and the platform serializes it. `exclusive_execution` joined `SHARED_CAPABILITIES` rather than the TTS vocabulary because it is a scheduling property of any `local` provider, not a TTS feature.

**B5 / K1 — the duplicate Kokoro engine is gone**
This was the substantive defect. `tts/routes.py` and `providers/kokoro/provider.py` each declared `kokoro_instance`, `kokoro_lock`, `generation_inference_lock`, and a misaki handle; only the route's copies were ever populated, so a caller reaching the provider could load a second ONNX session and serialize on a lock nobody else held. The provider package is now the sole owner, reached through `registry.get` rather than imported (B8 — the folder has no `__init__.py`). Routes and `pipeline/services.py` delegate. Note `load_model()` *rewrites* the voice catalog from the model, so `_voices()`/`_models()` are functions — a constant captured at import would have frozen the shipped defaults.

**Both providers on v2**
`TTSProvider` keeps its domain methods and gains `invoke(request, invocation) -> ProviderResult` with relative managed refs. Kokoro maps a missing model to `PROVIDER_NOT_CONFIGURED` and a bad voice to `PROVIDER_REQUEST_INVALID` instead of crashing. Inworld moved onto the shared direct-API transport; its old code raised `HTTPError(f"… — {resp.text[:200]}")` and a `RuntimeError` embedding the 404 body, putting third-party response text into persisted errors — `classify_http_error` never copies a body.

**Tests** moved to `tests/test_tts_providers.py`, following the precedent 14.2/14.3 set. A fixture provider runs the same generic suite as the shipped two, which is what makes it a contract suite rather than two implementation tests.

Two judgment calls worth flagging: I added `exclusive_execution` to a set contracts.md marks frozen (documented inline), and `download_model` still raises `NotImplementedError` while the manifest declares the `model_download` capability — the route performs the download, so the capability is truthful about the provider, but the method remains a gap I did not close here.

